1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,080
Multinationals have lower costs because they

2
00:00:04,080 --> 00:00:05,680
are better managed.

3
00:00:05,680 --> 00:00:09,440
And if they were allowed to, so I actually asked Ben Reed.

4
00:00:09,440 --> 00:00:13,120
And so back of a napkin, what if multinationals simply

5
00:00:13,120 --> 00:00:15,800
took over the entire world economy completely?

6
00:00:15,800 --> 00:00:18,400
What are your results say would happen?

7
00:00:18,400 --> 00:00:22,280
And he's like, global GDP rise is 50%.

8
00:00:22,280 --> 00:00:25,280
All right.

9
00:00:25,280 --> 00:00:27,360
And if you think about every country on Earth

10
00:00:27,360 --> 00:00:29,120
that has tried to keep multinationals out

11
00:00:29,120 --> 00:00:31,120
there's otherwise regarded them with suspicion.

12
00:00:31,120 --> 00:00:35,440
So I guess they're going to come here and take your inputs

13
00:00:35,440 --> 00:00:37,480
and turn them into a lot more outputs.

14
00:00:37,480 --> 00:00:43,000
And then of course, competition leads to a bunch of other things.

15
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:44,360
Anyway, all right.

16
00:00:44,360 --> 00:00:47,000
So questions about allocating reproductive efficiency?

17
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:48,400
I'll be there.

18
00:00:48,400 --> 00:00:52,760
All right, so probably the person that talked about this the most

19
00:00:52,760 --> 00:00:55,320
was actually Robert Bork in his book, The Antitrust Paradox,

20
00:00:55,320 --> 00:00:57,440
years before he was nominated to Supreme Court.

21
00:00:57,440 --> 00:01:01,320
So he has a lot of good stuff on that, all the outdated facts.

22
00:01:01,320 --> 00:01:02,720
In general, productive inefficiency

23
00:01:02,720 --> 00:01:04,720
just doesn't get much attention.

24
00:01:04,720 --> 00:01:07,000
I mean, probably some of it is there's just a theory of,

25
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:09,880
well, how could it be that any firm is ever producing

26
00:01:09,880 --> 00:01:12,040
at excessively high costs?

27
00:01:12,040 --> 00:01:13,760
There's the obvious point, what if it's

28
00:01:13,760 --> 00:01:15,920
illegal to produce at low costs?

29
00:01:15,920 --> 00:01:17,120
What about that?

30
00:01:17,120 --> 00:01:19,240
And then again, in the real world,

31
00:01:19,240 --> 00:01:22,040
there's always disequilibria where, for at least a while,

32
00:01:22,040 --> 00:01:24,240
firms probably might be doing things.

33
00:01:24,240 --> 00:01:25,280
Yeah, you had a question?

34
00:01:25,280 --> 00:01:28,360
I was wondering, what are the higher estimates of allocative

35
00:01:28,360 --> 00:01:28,860
efficiency?

36
00:01:28,860 --> 00:01:31,880
Yeah, so I think there was one paper that got up to 10%

37
00:01:31,880 --> 00:01:34,360
by assuming that every single industry in the United States

38
00:01:34,360 --> 00:01:35,800
was monopolized by one firm.

39
00:01:35,800 --> 00:01:36,300
Oh.

40
00:01:36,300 --> 00:01:39,280
Right, so.

41
00:01:39,280 --> 00:01:41,480
Yeah, so it only got to 10% as you can do that way.

42
00:01:41,480 --> 00:01:46,160
So you did, they had to make a very strict assumption.

43
00:01:46,160 --> 00:01:48,360
All right, moving on then.

44
00:01:48,360 --> 00:01:49,560
Everyone happy?

45
00:01:49,560 --> 00:01:53,040
Everyone full of energy for microeconomics?

46
00:01:53,040 --> 00:01:58,400
Everyone ready to ignore all signs of your biorhythms

47
00:01:58,400 --> 00:01:59,320
and the time of day?

48
00:01:59,320 --> 00:02:02,080
And all right.

49
00:02:02,080 --> 00:02:04,600
Right, of course, biorhythms are not important.

50
00:02:04,600 --> 00:02:06,320
This is not a biology class.

51
00:02:06,320 --> 00:02:07,160
This is economics.

52
00:02:07,160 --> 00:02:09,400
And the economy runs nonstop.

53
00:02:09,400 --> 00:02:11,240
And so must we.

54
00:02:11,240 --> 00:02:13,160
So continuing.

55
00:02:13,160 --> 00:02:16,360
All right, so predation, entry, deterrence, and mixed strategies.

56
00:02:16,360 --> 00:02:18,600
So predation means a lot of things to a lot of people.

57
00:02:18,600 --> 00:02:22,400
Is Amazon predating on the entire economy right now?

58
00:02:22,400 --> 00:02:24,040
Maybe.

59
00:02:24,040 --> 00:02:24,720
I don't know.

60
00:02:24,720 --> 00:02:26,440
What does the word even mean?

61
00:02:26,440 --> 00:02:28,680
So what insight can game theory give us

62
00:02:28,680 --> 00:02:32,880
into what predation might even mean or how much there is?

63
00:02:32,880 --> 00:02:35,840
So here's a very simple model of predation.

64
00:02:39,080 --> 00:02:40,440
And it just says, well, predation

65
00:02:40,440 --> 00:02:42,960
is the same thing as what is sometimes

66
00:02:42,960 --> 00:02:44,360
called limit pricing.

67
00:02:44,360 --> 00:02:46,740
And this just says, suppose there's a bunch of producers

68
00:02:46,740 --> 00:02:47,680
varying costs.

69
00:02:47,680 --> 00:02:49,080
And the lowest priced producer just

70
00:02:49,080 --> 00:02:52,440
prices just below the cost of the second most efficient

71
00:02:52,440 --> 00:02:54,800
producer.

72
00:02:54,800 --> 00:02:56,480
So imagine you've got a world like that.

73
00:02:56,480 --> 00:02:59,840
So we can go and here's demand.

74
00:02:59,840 --> 00:03:04,960
And then here's average cost firm 5, average cost firm 4,

75
00:03:04,960 --> 00:03:09,040
average cost firm 3, average cost firm 2,

76
00:03:09,040 --> 00:03:10,960
average cost firm 1.

77
00:03:10,960 --> 00:03:12,440
And if the most efficient producer

78
00:03:12,440 --> 00:03:15,880
goes just below those costs, then how many firms

79
00:03:15,880 --> 00:03:18,800
can actually profitably survive in the industry?

80
00:03:18,800 --> 00:03:19,300
One.

81
00:03:19,300 --> 00:03:20,600
Just one.

82
00:03:20,600 --> 00:03:22,840
And how did it occur?

83
00:03:22,840 --> 00:03:27,280
He set prices so low that everyone else either died

84
00:03:27,280 --> 00:03:30,520
or just was never even born.

85
00:03:30,520 --> 00:03:32,480
He made the other firms wish they had never

86
00:03:32,480 --> 00:03:37,120
been born by going and setting the price so low.

87
00:03:37,120 --> 00:03:41,600
Now, this kind of thing probably happens a lot.

88
00:03:41,600 --> 00:03:43,640
And a lot of the question is just, well,

89
00:03:43,640 --> 00:03:46,080
are they even trying to do it?

90
00:03:46,080 --> 00:03:48,720
So who cares whether they're trying to do it or not?

91
00:03:48,720 --> 00:03:50,080
Lawyers care.

92
00:03:50,080 --> 00:03:52,080
Juries care.

93
00:03:52,080 --> 00:03:53,720
You are much more likely to get convicted

94
00:03:53,720 --> 00:03:56,240
if there's a single memo that they can dig out

95
00:03:56,240 --> 00:04:00,240
of your archives saying, let's destroy the competition.

96
00:04:00,240 --> 00:04:02,920
Let's drive them into the ground forever.

97
00:04:02,920 --> 00:04:05,280
Whereas you simply offer a great product

98
00:04:05,280 --> 00:04:09,120
at a low price, which causes the very same destruction.

99
00:04:09,120 --> 00:04:10,680
But there's no memo.

100
00:04:10,680 --> 00:04:12,880
It's like, look, we don't want to hurt anybody.

101
00:04:12,880 --> 00:04:16,480
We just want to give everybody a fantastic deal.

102
00:04:16,480 --> 00:04:19,720
Yes, but that will automatically cause destruction

103
00:04:19,720 --> 00:04:21,040
of your competitors.

104
00:04:21,040 --> 00:04:23,240
Well, that's just a theory.

105
00:04:23,240 --> 00:04:24,040
I don't know.

106
00:04:24,040 --> 00:04:26,720
I don't want to hurt anybody.

107
00:04:26,720 --> 00:04:28,840
I'm just trying to help people.

108
00:04:28,840 --> 00:04:30,640
That's all I want to do.

109
00:04:30,640 --> 00:04:33,960
So it matters a lot in the eyes of the law.

110
00:04:33,960 --> 00:04:34,960
I didn't.

111
00:04:34,960 --> 00:04:36,640
Why is it so important?

112
00:04:36,640 --> 00:04:40,840
Well, that's a good question.

113
00:04:40,840 --> 00:04:44,000
Does it matter if you destroyed all your competitors

114
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:45,560
because you were trying to do it,

115
00:04:45,560 --> 00:04:47,120
or if it just happened inadvertently

116
00:04:47,120 --> 00:04:49,520
as a result of offering a great product at low prices?

117
00:04:52,080 --> 00:04:53,560
But predatory intent is a lot more

118
00:04:53,560 --> 00:04:55,560
likely to cost you a lot of money.

119
00:04:55,560 --> 00:04:57,880
So it's important for that reason.

120
00:04:57,880 --> 00:04:59,920
And again, if this were a law in economics class,

121
00:04:59,920 --> 00:05:01,320
we'd be going into this more.

122
00:05:01,320 --> 00:05:04,440
So there is sort of an interesting question,

123
00:05:04,440 --> 00:05:09,200
like why is it that we punish murder more harshly

124
00:05:09,200 --> 00:05:11,920
than attempted murder?

125
00:05:11,920 --> 00:05:12,800
So what's the deal?

126
00:05:12,800 --> 00:05:15,680
Yeah, why not determine both equally?

127
00:05:15,680 --> 00:05:17,160
What's the difference?

128
00:05:17,160 --> 00:05:19,200
And the best story anyone in law economics

129
00:05:19,200 --> 00:05:22,520
ever come up with is, well, if you punish murder,

130
00:05:22,520 --> 00:05:24,440
attempted murder equally harshly,

131
00:05:24,440 --> 00:05:27,240
then the attempted murderer has a clear incentive

132
00:05:27,240 --> 00:05:29,120
to try to finish the job if he screws up

133
00:05:29,120 --> 00:05:31,280
the first time around.

134
00:05:31,280 --> 00:05:33,920
All right, so how many of you have witnessed murders

135
00:05:33,920 --> 00:05:36,360
where they are like, uh-oh.

136
00:05:36,360 --> 00:05:38,560
No, like there are actually maybe a few.

137
00:05:38,560 --> 00:05:41,320
So maybe like hit men, for example.

138
00:05:41,320 --> 00:05:43,120
I might be calculating that carefully.

139
00:05:43,120 --> 00:05:47,080
That's even a little bit hard to believe.

140
00:05:47,080 --> 00:05:49,080
So this is one model, which is something

141
00:05:49,080 --> 00:05:52,480
you can call predation if you want to.

142
00:05:52,480 --> 00:05:54,080
Should it be called predation or not,

143
00:05:54,080 --> 00:05:57,600
I'll just leave that to your judgment.

144
00:05:57,600 --> 00:05:59,400
Now, a more interesting model of predation

145
00:05:59,400 --> 00:06:00,600
is something more like this.

146
00:06:00,600 --> 00:06:04,000
There's an incumbent, and he has a conditional pricing strategy,

147
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:06,440
which he prices high if there's no entrance,

148
00:06:06,440 --> 00:06:08,120
and low if there's an entrance.

149
00:06:08,120 --> 00:06:10,800
And then the entrance size is whether to enter.

150
00:06:10,800 --> 00:06:13,200
And you might remember we even talked about this situation

151
00:06:13,200 --> 00:06:15,160
before.

152
00:06:15,160 --> 00:06:17,600
So if you have a situation like this,

153
00:06:17,600 --> 00:06:20,840
then we've got two Nash equilibrium.

154
00:06:20,840 --> 00:06:22,960
So we've got our first Nash equilibrium,

155
00:06:22,960 --> 00:06:26,800
the Nash equilibrium out fight, which

156
00:06:26,800 --> 00:06:29,360
means that the entrant first goes and enters.

157
00:06:29,360 --> 00:06:31,920
Or the entrant doesn't enter.

158
00:06:31,920 --> 00:06:36,320
And then the incumbent would have gone and drastically cut

159
00:06:36,320 --> 00:06:38,480
prices if the entrant had entered,

160
00:06:38,480 --> 00:06:41,440
and therefore, neither wants to change its behavior.

161
00:06:41,440 --> 00:06:44,040
Because if you know that the incumbent would have cut its

162
00:06:44,040 --> 00:06:46,120
prices if you entered, you don't want to enter.

163
00:06:46,120 --> 00:06:48,720
And if the entrant is staying out,

164
00:06:48,720 --> 00:06:53,160
you don't want to change what you would do if he came in.

165
00:06:53,160 --> 00:06:55,640
And then there's the inaccommodate one as well.

166
00:06:55,640 --> 00:06:58,160
Inaccommodate, so that's where the entrant does go in,

167
00:06:58,160 --> 00:07:03,880
and then the incumbent goes and actually reduces output

168
00:07:03,880 --> 00:07:06,120
to accommodate the new entrant.

169
00:07:06,120 --> 00:07:08,640
So that's where you actually cut back on your production

170
00:07:08,640 --> 00:07:10,240
because it's new entrant, and you now

171
00:07:10,240 --> 00:07:12,560
need to share the market.

172
00:07:12,560 --> 00:07:14,680
And you may remember one of these two outcomes

173
00:07:14,680 --> 00:07:16,560
is not sub-key perfect, because it

174
00:07:16,560 --> 00:07:17,960
involves a threat to do something

175
00:07:17,960 --> 00:07:19,560
you wouldn't want to do if you actually

176
00:07:19,560 --> 00:07:21,600
had to carry it out.

177
00:07:21,600 --> 00:07:24,520
Which one of these two is not sub-key perfect?

178
00:07:24,520 --> 00:07:25,520
Out fight.

179
00:07:25,520 --> 00:07:28,060
So you come in there, and you say, well, now that you're here,

180
00:07:28,060 --> 00:07:29,880
I'm going to destroy us both.

181
00:07:29,880 --> 00:07:30,920
It's like, hmm.

182
00:07:30,920 --> 00:07:32,880
I mean, it's one thing to say, if you come in,

183
00:07:32,880 --> 00:07:34,040
I'll destroy us both.

184
00:07:34,040 --> 00:07:34,960
That's kind of scary.

185
00:07:34,960 --> 00:07:36,480
Once you're in, now destroy us both.

186
00:07:36,480 --> 00:07:36,980
Really?

187
00:07:36,980 --> 00:07:38,600
You're really going to destroy us both?

188
00:07:38,600 --> 00:07:41,160
Wouldn't you rather just cooperate with me?

189
00:07:41,160 --> 00:07:43,600
Oh, don't want to.

190
00:07:43,600 --> 00:07:47,960
Cooperating with an entrant is so humiliating.

191
00:07:47,960 --> 00:07:49,120
It's a daffy duck.

192
00:07:49,120 --> 00:07:50,920
Daffy duck is so humiliating.

193
00:07:50,920 --> 00:07:51,800
I'd rather cooperate.

194
00:07:54,640 --> 00:07:56,520
So anyway, this is a case where the incumbent

195
00:07:56,520 --> 00:07:58,520
would be cutting off his nose to spite his face.

196
00:07:58,520 --> 00:08:00,400
It doesn't make a lot of sense.

197
00:08:00,400 --> 00:08:04,000
Now, there is a less formal literature on predation.

198
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:07,280
It's like an earlier one from the 50s and 60s and 70s,

199
00:08:07,280 --> 00:08:10,400
which adds on and says, on top of all this,

200
00:08:10,400 --> 00:08:14,000
not only is fighting a bad idea, but fighting is actually

201
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:16,080
especially bad for the incumbent.

202
00:08:16,080 --> 00:08:18,560
Because normally, which firm has the bigger market share,

203
00:08:18,560 --> 00:08:21,120
the incumbent or the entrant?

204
00:08:21,120 --> 00:08:23,160
The incumbent normally has a bigger market share.

205
00:08:23,160 --> 00:08:28,760
So if you go and slash prices down to a level

206
00:08:28,760 --> 00:08:31,960
where you're losing money, and you have 90% of the market,

207
00:08:31,960 --> 00:08:35,840
then you'll be losing nine times as much money as the entrant.

208
00:08:35,840 --> 00:08:38,760
It sounds like it's like cutting your face nine times

209
00:08:38,760 --> 00:08:41,240
to go and cut the entrant's face one time.

210
00:08:41,240 --> 00:08:43,240
It doesn't sound very smart.

211
00:08:43,240 --> 00:08:45,120
And actually, even that's not right.

212
00:08:45,120 --> 00:08:52,160
Because suppose that if you didn't go and cut your price

213
00:08:52,160 --> 00:08:55,840
response, you'd have 90%, and the entrant would have 5%,

214
00:08:55,840 --> 00:08:57,280
and the entrant would have 10%.

215
00:08:57,280 --> 00:09:05,000
So suppose you keep output the same, then the divide is 90-10.

216
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:07,920
But if the incumbent actually wants to fight,

217
00:09:07,920 --> 00:09:10,360
he's got to go and do what with his output?

218
00:09:10,360 --> 00:09:10,960
Increase it.

219
00:09:10,960 --> 00:09:11,480
Increase it.

220
00:09:11,480 --> 00:09:15,600
So then that might mean going up to 95% of the industry.

221
00:09:15,600 --> 00:09:18,280
So then you're losing 19 times as much money

222
00:09:18,280 --> 00:09:20,560
as the entrant, which again doesn't

223
00:09:20,560 --> 00:09:22,920
sound like a very good deal.

224
00:09:22,920 --> 00:09:26,800
So this is a vaguely related literature

225
00:09:26,800 --> 00:09:29,880
to saying that actually, since the real world incumbents usually

226
00:09:29,880 --> 00:09:33,440
are the market leader, they lose more money from predation

227
00:09:33,440 --> 00:09:35,560
than the entrant would.

228
00:09:35,560 --> 00:09:37,400
But anyway, the game theory point

229
00:09:37,400 --> 00:09:39,520
is just that regardless of that, it's

230
00:09:39,520 --> 00:09:41,600
cheaper to accommodate than to fight.

231
00:09:41,600 --> 00:09:44,560
And so incumbents want to do that.

232
00:09:44,560 --> 00:09:52,160
Now, all of this is, of course, forgetting about the first word

233
00:09:52,160 --> 00:09:52,720
of the notes.

234
00:09:52,720 --> 00:09:54,120
What is the first word?

235
00:09:54,120 --> 00:09:58,440
Let's double check the first word of the notes.

236
00:09:58,440 --> 00:09:59,840
Yes.

237
00:09:59,840 --> 00:10:02,360
So after the colon, yes, repeating.

238
00:10:02,360 --> 00:10:06,600
So all of these arguments are ignoring repeated play.

239
00:10:06,600 --> 00:10:10,560
So what if this same game is infinitely repeated?

240
00:10:10,560 --> 00:10:14,440
And the simple answer is, well, then predation might work.

241
00:10:14,440 --> 00:10:17,880
So why can it work to go and destroy money?

242
00:10:17,880 --> 00:10:20,280
Well, it's partially destroy both of us

243
00:10:20,280 --> 00:10:23,680
if there's an infinitely repeated game.

244
00:10:23,680 --> 00:10:27,640
So I go and I blow up all our profits for this period.

245
00:10:27,640 --> 00:10:29,920
It's kind of like reputation.

246
00:10:29,920 --> 00:10:31,280
Yeah, exactly.

247
00:10:31,280 --> 00:10:34,440
Walmart has some of its people who try to unionize,

248
00:10:34,440 --> 00:10:37,040
or shut down the whole store and just give up

249
00:10:37,040 --> 00:10:39,280
all the profits on that whole store

250
00:10:39,280 --> 00:10:41,280
to break the back of the union members

251
00:10:41,280 --> 00:10:45,560
so that it has a reputation that it's worth its time.

252
00:10:45,560 --> 00:10:47,920
Sounds pretty clever.

253
00:10:47,920 --> 00:10:50,720
Want to try it?

254
00:10:50,720 --> 00:10:52,160
All right, yes, so that's the idea.

255
00:10:52,160 --> 00:10:54,040
It's like, look, you might actually

256
00:10:54,040 --> 00:10:56,880
want to let one of your stores go down the drain

257
00:10:56,880 --> 00:10:59,560
if this is going to wind up giving you

258
00:10:59,560 --> 00:11:02,160
other benefits in the future.

259
00:11:02,160 --> 00:11:05,880
So anyway, so it is possible that with infinite repetition,

260
00:11:05,880 --> 00:11:08,120
then predation would be successful.

261
00:11:08,120 --> 00:11:10,840
And that's when arguments like this become important.

262
00:11:10,840 --> 00:11:12,840
That's when arguments like that become important,

263
00:11:12,840 --> 00:11:15,840
because it's like, you're going to go and build a reputation

264
00:11:15,840 --> 00:11:20,000
by losing 19 times as much money as the competition for a while.

265
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:22,240
That's a great plan.

266
00:11:22,240 --> 00:11:24,200
That's probably a stupid plan.

267
00:11:24,200 --> 00:11:26,440
It's the kind of thing where you might actually

268
00:11:26,440 --> 00:11:30,040
lead competitors to say, wow, you lost so much money.

269
00:11:30,040 --> 00:11:32,480
I don't think I'll ever try that dumb move again.

270
00:11:32,480 --> 00:11:35,200
Now I really don't believe him anymore.

271
00:11:35,200 --> 00:11:38,880
So it could easily lead to that reaction.

272
00:11:38,880 --> 00:11:41,880
Now, there is a substantial literature on, especially

273
00:11:41,880 --> 00:11:46,840
the 19th century economic history, which probably gets

274
00:11:46,840 --> 00:11:49,520
more attention, because back then,

275
00:11:49,520 --> 00:11:53,280
there was no legal reason not to admit you were doing predation.

276
00:11:53,280 --> 00:11:56,720
And the general result of people who have looked at this,

277
00:11:56,720 --> 00:12:00,040
as far as I know, I haven't looked closely,

278
00:12:00,040 --> 00:12:03,080
but Don McGraw would actually know this quite well,

279
00:12:03,080 --> 00:12:06,040
is that Rockefeller, for example,

280
00:12:06,040 --> 00:12:07,720
so Rockefeller is actually, or the Citadel

281
00:12:07,720 --> 00:12:10,240
is the most famous one for supposedly engaging predation.

282
00:12:10,240 --> 00:12:12,200
The people that have looked at it most closely

283
00:12:12,200 --> 00:12:14,360
came away saying, no, no, in an unengaged predation,

284
00:12:14,360 --> 00:12:16,440
he did something that's almost the opposite of that,

285
00:12:16,440 --> 00:12:18,320
which is he would go and buy competitors out

286
00:12:18,320 --> 00:12:20,280
at above market rates.

287
00:12:20,280 --> 00:12:21,720
So he did do that.

288
00:12:21,720 --> 00:12:25,520
So going and just slashing the price of kerosene

289
00:12:25,520 --> 00:12:29,000
for a long time to drive people out, that, no sign that he did

290
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:29,760
it.

291
00:12:29,760 --> 00:12:32,440
But what he would do is go to firms and say, look,

292
00:12:32,440 --> 00:12:34,840
I'll go and pay you double the value of your assets

293
00:12:34,840 --> 00:12:36,400
to sell me.

294
00:12:36,400 --> 00:12:39,400
And then, my memory is that we actually

295
00:12:39,400 --> 00:12:41,360
have the smoking gun of Rockefeller,

296
00:12:41,360 --> 00:12:43,360
then saying, we have to stop doing this,

297
00:12:43,360 --> 00:12:46,040
because people are building new refineries simply

298
00:12:46,040 --> 00:12:49,200
to be bought out at double the price of the refinery.

299
00:12:49,200 --> 00:12:53,280
So he calls it blackmailing us, and then he

300
00:12:53,280 --> 00:12:55,000
just gave up on that.

301
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:59,440
So not much historical sign of predation in this sense,

302
00:12:59,440 --> 00:13:02,000
where you deliberately are losing money for a while

303
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:08,760
in order to scare people off.

304
00:13:08,760 --> 00:13:10,360
And in the case of the union there,

305
00:13:10,360 --> 00:13:12,520
you say, well, you just have to shut down one store,

306
00:13:12,520 --> 00:13:14,480
and that can scare a ton of people.

307
00:13:14,480 --> 00:13:19,400
But if you had to go and lose a ton of money for the whole,

308
00:13:19,400 --> 00:13:21,480
all of Walmart for years, possibly,

309
00:13:21,480 --> 00:13:23,560
then there's other considerations people want out.

310
00:13:23,560 --> 00:13:25,400
Like, suppose there's a durable good,

311
00:13:25,400 --> 00:13:29,080
and you're concerned that the price is only temporarily low.

312
00:13:29,080 --> 00:13:32,400
What might you as a consumer do?

313
00:13:32,400 --> 00:13:33,800
Yes, stock up.

314
00:13:33,800 --> 00:13:36,360
So then you might have to expand your output massively

315
00:13:36,360 --> 00:13:40,000
to deal with all the people who are stockpiling the good.

316
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:41,680
Imagine you were, say, going to try

317
00:13:41,680 --> 00:13:44,360
doing predation in automobiles by cutting the price

318
00:13:44,360 --> 00:13:45,960
to half the normal price.

319
00:13:45,960 --> 00:13:47,960
How many people would want to just go and buy,

320
00:13:47,960 --> 00:13:52,600
oh, let's go and buy cars for all of our kids right now.

321
00:13:52,600 --> 00:13:55,640
Depending upon how much land you have.

322
00:13:55,640 --> 00:13:58,040
If you're in New York City, you probably wouldn't do that.

323
00:13:58,040 --> 00:13:59,840
But if you're out in the country, you've got a barn,

324
00:13:59,840 --> 00:14:01,920
put them in there, put a car cover on them,

325
00:14:01,920 --> 00:14:04,280
start them once a month, and it's a pretty good deal.

326
00:14:08,240 --> 00:14:10,800
Now, a big question about this kind of predation

327
00:14:10,800 --> 00:14:13,320
is, why couldn't everybody do it?

328
00:14:13,320 --> 00:14:15,480
So why would it have to be limited, actually,

329
00:14:15,480 --> 00:14:17,000
to incumbents?

330
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:19,160
Why couldn't entrants predict?

331
00:14:19,160 --> 00:14:21,400
So you could have an entrant say, tell you what,

332
00:14:21,400 --> 00:14:24,280
if you don't just flee from this industry right now,

333
00:14:24,280 --> 00:14:26,680
I'm going to come in and I'm going to go and make us lose

334
00:14:26,680 --> 00:14:30,200
a ton of money.

335
00:14:30,200 --> 00:14:33,480
And that seems structurally very similar to, if you enter,

336
00:14:33,480 --> 00:14:35,200
I'm going to make us both lose money.

337
00:14:35,200 --> 00:14:37,160
And say, look, if you don't run away,

338
00:14:37,160 --> 00:14:39,760
I am going to make us both lose money.

339
00:14:39,760 --> 00:14:42,840
So it seems like a principle that could happen.

340
00:14:42,840 --> 00:14:45,240
And this seems very odd to imagine, say,

341
00:14:45,240 --> 00:14:48,200
like one small chicken shack threatening

342
00:14:48,200 --> 00:14:51,120
to do this to Kentucky Fried Chicken or something like that.

343
00:14:51,120 --> 00:14:53,280
You can imagine large-scale entry,

344
00:14:53,280 --> 00:14:55,280
where, say, McDonald's might say,

345
00:14:55,280 --> 00:14:59,520
we're going to open up our own line of chicken restaurants.

346
00:14:59,520 --> 00:15:03,080
And there are some interesting cases of large-scale entry.

347
00:15:03,080 --> 00:15:08,720
For example, Microsoft came along with the Xbox.

348
00:15:08,720 --> 00:15:11,840
So it wasn't just like some small video game company

349
00:15:11,840 --> 00:15:13,200
out of somebody's garage.

350
00:15:13,200 --> 00:15:15,600
Microsoft said, we're going to go and enter

351
00:15:15,600 --> 00:15:19,360
this console video game industry on a massive scale.

352
00:15:19,360 --> 00:15:20,840
That's what they did.

353
00:15:20,840 --> 00:15:24,560
And so Amazon, remember what Amazon used to sell only?

354
00:15:24,560 --> 00:15:25,060
Books.

355
00:15:25,060 --> 00:15:26,920
They only sold books.

356
00:15:26,920 --> 00:15:29,320
But there's one of the biggest booksellers.

357
00:15:29,320 --> 00:15:32,720
And now they start selling, we'll also sell reflecting

358
00:15:32,720 --> 00:15:33,720
flashers.

359
00:15:33,720 --> 00:15:35,680
And we'll make you attach some to your car

360
00:15:35,680 --> 00:15:37,020
so you don't need them at night.

361
00:15:37,020 --> 00:15:38,360
We'll also do that.

362
00:15:38,360 --> 00:15:41,360
So that's possible as well.

363
00:15:41,360 --> 00:15:44,160
So anyway, so why can't entrants bring it against incumbents,

364
00:15:44,160 --> 00:15:46,440
just like incumbents bring it against entrants?

365
00:15:46,440 --> 00:15:48,080
And when you start thinking about this,

366
00:15:48,080 --> 00:15:50,640
then remember that mixed strategy concept

367
00:15:50,640 --> 00:15:52,920
that we had before?

368
00:15:52,920 --> 00:15:55,960
Maybe you could actually have every possible kind

369
00:15:55,960 --> 00:15:57,000
of prediction.

370
00:15:57,000 --> 00:15:59,360
You could have incumbents bringing it against entrants,

371
00:15:59,360 --> 00:16:01,200
entrants bringing it against incumbents.

372
00:16:01,200 --> 00:16:03,720
You would have cases where they both go in,

373
00:16:03,720 --> 00:16:06,600
thinking that the other one would be scared off.

374
00:16:06,600 --> 00:16:08,960
And you can also have a case where everyone is so scared

375
00:16:08,960 --> 00:16:11,040
that they're going to lose money that they don't go in the industry

376
00:16:11,040 --> 00:16:11,540
at all.

377
00:16:15,320 --> 00:16:18,160
So anyway, the idea in these cases

378
00:16:18,160 --> 00:16:20,840
is that in addition to the pure strategy

379
00:16:20,840 --> 00:16:22,600
Nash equilibrium with one brigading

380
00:16:22,600 --> 00:16:24,320
and the other one staying away, you've

381
00:16:24,320 --> 00:16:26,480
also got the mixed strategy Nash equilibrium

382
00:16:26,480 --> 00:16:28,840
of every possible combination happening

383
00:16:28,840 --> 00:16:31,720
with some probability.

384
00:16:31,720 --> 00:16:33,720
Intuitively, what has to happen is

385
00:16:33,720 --> 00:16:35,800
that the potential for monopoly profits,

386
00:16:35,800 --> 00:16:37,560
if you get to be the monopoly, have

387
00:16:37,560 --> 00:16:40,200
to balance out the expected losses

388
00:16:40,200 --> 00:16:44,280
from this destructive competition.

389
00:16:44,280 --> 00:16:46,360
You've heard this phrase, destructive competition

390
00:16:46,360 --> 00:16:47,520
at some point?

391
00:16:47,520 --> 00:16:51,400
Destructive competition.

392
00:16:51,400 --> 00:16:52,800
Of course, you could just use this

393
00:16:52,800 --> 00:16:55,400
for as a synonym for competition.

394
00:16:55,400 --> 00:16:58,160
All competition is destructive as far as I'm concerned.

395
00:16:58,160 --> 00:17:01,080
But normally, when outsiders, observers describe it,

396
00:17:01,080 --> 00:17:04,520
this is where firms that have substantial fixed costs

397
00:17:04,520 --> 00:17:07,000
wind up driving prices down to marginal cost

398
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:10,160
and then they lose money.

399
00:17:10,160 --> 00:17:11,120
All right.

400
00:17:11,120 --> 00:17:13,000
So anyway, say that the mixed strategy story

401
00:17:13,000 --> 00:17:14,840
does make more sense.

402
00:17:14,840 --> 00:17:19,320
So again, there's no way you can credibly commit to be in no

403
00:17:19,320 --> 00:17:20,920
matter what.

404
00:17:20,920 --> 00:17:24,160
So the bigger the conditional benefit of being monopoly

405
00:17:24,160 --> 00:17:25,800
is, the harder firms will compete

406
00:17:25,800 --> 00:17:28,560
to get to be that person.

407
00:17:28,560 --> 00:17:31,000
Which you can probably see a lot of this on the internet.

408
00:17:31,000 --> 00:17:32,760
So remember in the early days, there

409
00:17:32,760 --> 00:17:34,680
were a bunch of firms that actually really did

410
00:17:34,680 --> 00:17:36,600
seem to be losing a ton of money.

411
00:17:36,600 --> 00:17:39,360
Strangely, no one complained about it.

412
00:17:39,360 --> 00:17:41,440
Again, if there's ever a case where you just say,

413
00:17:41,440 --> 00:17:44,280
predation smoking gun, wouldn't be a whole industry

414
00:17:44,280 --> 00:17:47,240
where most of the firms were losing money?

415
00:17:47,240 --> 00:17:50,240
And again, you might say, well, maybe that'd be a hard time.

416
00:17:50,240 --> 00:17:51,920
No.

417
00:17:51,920 --> 00:17:54,840
They show up and start losing money immediately,

418
00:17:54,840 --> 00:17:58,240
and they lose money for years.

419
00:17:58,240 --> 00:18:00,240
It seems a lot like predation.

420
00:18:00,240 --> 00:18:02,440
Now, in practice, fortunately, the law just

421
00:18:02,440 --> 00:18:04,200
turned a blind eye to this.

422
00:18:04,200 --> 00:18:08,320
And anyway, why would the law turn a blind eye to this?

423
00:18:08,320 --> 00:18:11,440
Well, how do laws get enforced in the real world normally?

424
00:18:11,440 --> 00:18:12,840
AUDIENCE MEMBER 5 In the courts.

425
00:18:12,840 --> 00:18:17,560
Well, but how does something even get to the courts?

426
00:18:17,560 --> 00:18:20,600
Someone has to complain.

427
00:18:20,600 --> 00:18:22,600
Someone has to complain.

428
00:18:22,600 --> 00:18:25,000
And if there's an industry that's changing real rapidly,

429
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:26,420
by the time you get to the courts,

430
00:18:26,420 --> 00:18:28,160
the whole industry may change anyway.

431
00:18:28,160 --> 00:18:31,360
So that's a lot of what I saw during the early days

432
00:18:31,360 --> 00:18:33,760
of the internet, is firms that just went in and lost

433
00:18:33,760 --> 00:18:35,760
a ton of money, and then actually most of them

434
00:18:35,760 --> 00:18:37,200
just went bankrupt.

435
00:18:37,200 --> 00:18:40,320
So I guess.

436
00:18:40,320 --> 00:18:43,360
So you listed six reasons in the past six minutes

437
00:18:43,360 --> 00:18:46,080
of why predation isn't a good idea.

438
00:18:46,080 --> 00:18:49,080
And you gave an example of Rockefeller

439
00:18:49,080 --> 00:18:50,480
doing the exact opposite.

440
00:18:50,480 --> 00:18:53,720
So if he wasn't doing it, and it's a bad idea to do it,

